<BaseLayout title="About LiquidityPool.app" description="LiquidityPool.app is an independent, educational research library explaining the mechanics and risks of decentralized liquidity.">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow eyebrow--mint">About the publication</div><h1 class="page-title">Research for a market that moves too fast for shortcuts.</h1><p>LiquidityPool.app is an independent educational library for people who want to understand how decentralized liquidity behaves before they rely on a quote, an APR, or a dashboard.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section section--paper"><div class="container manifesto"><p class="lead">Good research does not manufacture certainty. It makes uncertainty specific enough to test.</p><p>Liquidity pools sit at the center of much of DeFi, yet the common language around them is often compressed into yield, APR, or the latest incentive program. This publication begins with the mechanism instead: who supplies inventory, how a trade changes reserves, where a position becomes inactive, and which risks remain after a headline return is removed.</p><p>Our launch library focuses on recurring concepts rather than a daily feed. Each guide is written to help readers connect an observable pool state to a decision question. The site does not publish price targets, token endorsements, or performance promises.</p><div class="principles"><div class="principle"><span>01</span><h2>Mechanism first</h2><p>We explain the moving parts before discussing outcomes. A position only makes sense in the context of the market structure that created it.</p></div><div class="principle"><span>02</span><h2>Sources in view</h2><p>Material factual claims are cited to primary protocol documentation, open research, or institutional publications. Every guide ends with a reference list and review date.</p></div><div class="principle"><span>03</span><h2>Risk in the argument</h2><p>Price movement, smart-contract design, incentives, asset dependencies, and execution conditions belong in the main argument—not in fine print.</p></div></div><h2 class="display" style="margin-top:72px">How the library is maintained</h2><p>Guides are reviewed when a material protocol mechanism, security assumption, or linked primary source changes. We correct substantive errors by updating the article, its sources, and the visible review date. The publication uses responsibly sourced editorial imagery with visible creator and license attribution.</p><p>LiquidityPool.app is educational and general in nature. Nothing here is investment, legal, tax, or financial advice. Readers should independently verify changing protocol parameters and assess whether they understand a position’s mechanism before signing a transaction.</p></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
